+++ b/cli-config.yaml.example
@@ -875,6 +875,12 @@ prompt_caching:
 #   # per session. Unset = 3 inside delegated subagents, unlimited for the main
 #   # agent; a number applies everywhere; 0 = unlimited.
 #   max_calls_per_image: 3
+#   # Images one request may carry before the oldest tool-result screenshots
+#   # are retired in batches (clamped 4..100). 20 matches Anthropic's
+#   # stricter-dimension threshold; raise it on providers without that cap.
+#   outbound_image_limit: 20
+#   # Image bytes one request may carry before the same retirement (1..30 MB).
+#   outbound_image_budget_mb: 24
 #
 # auxiliary:
 #   # Image analysis: vision_analyze tool + browser screenshots
diff --git a/website/docs/user-guide/features/vision.md b/website/docs/user-guide/features/vision.md
index 4e777c42e3..1b489bd785 100644
--- a/website/docs/user-guide/features/vision.md
+++ b/website/docs/user-guide/features/vision.md
@@ -227,6 +227,18 @@ For text-only main models (or providers whose tool-result channel doesn't carry
 
 Responses-style backends (for example `openai-codex`) accept only inline JPEG, PNG, GIF and WebP; any other `data:image/*` part makes them reject the **whole** request, and because the part stays in history every later turn fails the same way. Hermes handles this at the send layer: an inline **SVG** is rasterized to PNG when a rasterizer is installed (`cairosvg`, `svglib`+`reportlab`, `rsvg-convert`, or `inkscape` — the same soft dependencies `vision_analyze` uses), so the model still sees the drawing. Without a rasterizer, an SVG — and any other unsupported inline format such as BMP or TIFF — is replaced by a short text placeholder (`[image omitted: image/svg+xml is not a supported image format]`) while the valid images in the same message are still sent.
 
+### How many images a request carries: `vision.outbound_image_limit` and `vision.outbound_image_budget_mb`
+
+Screenshots from `computer_use` and the browser tools stay in history until a request would cross a provider image limit. Hermes then replaces the oldest tool-result screenshots with a `[screenshot removed to save context]` placeholder, a batch at a time, so the prompt cache survives between batches. Images you upload yourself are never removed.
+
+```yaml
+vision:
+  outbound_image_limit: 20      # images per request before retirement; clamped 4..100 (default 20)
+  outbound_image_budget_mb: 24  # image bytes per request before retirement; clamped 1..30 (default 24)
+```
+
+The default of 20 is where Anthropic starts applying a stricter per-image dimension cap to every image in the request. On Gemini or OpenAI models, or when your screenshots are already small, raise `outbound_image_limit` (say `60`) so long computer-use sessions keep more frames in view. Both send paths (the OpenAI-shaped request and the Anthropic wire request) read the same values, so they always retire the same screenshots.
+
 ### Native embeds ride the session: `vision.embed_target_bytes` and `vision.max_calls_per_image`
 
 A native `vision_analyze` result bakes the image into the tool result, and that result is re-sent on every later API call of the session. Two `config.yaml` keys bound the recurring cost:
